<TrustPage title="About CalcsDesk | Calculators that show their working" description="What CalcsDesk is building, which calculators are available in beta, and how sources, testing and review are handled." breadcrumb="About" eyebrow="About the desk" heading="A clearer view of the working." intro="CalcsDesk is being built to help people understand money calculations in their own country. The aim is to make the inputs, assumptions and steps as easy to inspect as the final number.">
  <div class="trust-callout"><strong>Where the site stands today</strong><p>The first tool is a limited <a href="/us/tax/income-tax-calculator/">2026 US federal income-tax beta</a>. It covers wage income with the basic standard deduction and shows employee Social Security and Medicare separately. Human review and the remaining release checks are pending. The collection also has a <a href="/uk/pay/salary-calculator/">UK monthly-salary beta</a> and a <a href="/ae/gratuity/gratuity-calculator/">limited UAE gratuity beta</a>, each with its own supported case and review status. A <a href="/in/tax/income-tax-calculator/">limited India salary-regime comparison</a> shows both modeled routes for the current tax year.</p></div>
  <h2>Start with the place that matters.</h2><p>A calculator’s scope should be clear before you use it. CalcsDesk organises the planned collection by country, then by the task. The home page and country pages distinguish the working beta from tools still in preparation. A planned calculator is not presented as an available estimate.</p>
  <h2>Explain the number.</h2><p>The federal beta shows the taxable-income calculation, the amount inside each tax band, and the employee payroll-tax components. It also lists what it does not model. The annual estimate is separate from a monthly average; neither is described as a payroll-withholding calculation or a complete tax return.</p><p>Read the assumptions beside the inputs. If your situation falls outside them, use the relevant official service or seek help from a qualified professional.</p>
  <h2>Make the evidence visible.</h2><p>Changeable rate data belongs with a source URL, an effective period and a check date. The beta links to the IRS and Social Security Administration references used for its 2026 data. A dated source check is not the same as a professional review. We show that distinction rather than implying a sign-off that has not happened.</p><p>Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> explains the intended release checks, while the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> describes sources and AI assistance.</p>
  <h2>Keep the task straightforward.</h2><p>The beta calculations run in the browser without submitting financial figures to a server. It does not require an account. If you choose to copy a federal estimate link, the figures and filing status are included in its URL fragment; the calculator explains this before you share it.</p>
  <h2>Funding and independence.</h2><p>The current site has no ad integration or affiliate links. If those are introduced later, they will need separate approval and clear labelling. A future funding arrangement must not be described as already operating.</p>
  <h2>What comes next.</h2><p>The wider collection is still being built. Availability, rate changes and important corrections belong in the public <a href="/updates/">updates log</a>. A reporting form is also being prepared; the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a> makes its current availability clear.</p>
</TrustPage>
